
=== Van Luijk discriminant test ===

At p = 3:
  Frobenius data (t_1, t_2, t_3) = (38, 166, 278)
  Algebraic eigenvalues: 16 of +3, 4 of -3 (rho = 20)
  Transcendental pair: α = 1 + 2i√2, |α|² = 9
  Trans char poly: f_3(T) = 1 - 2T + 9T²
  disc(f_3) = -32  (= -2^5)
  Square class: -2

At p = 11:
  Frobenius data (t_1, t_2) = (118, 2374)
  Algebraic eigenvalues: 15 of +11, 5 of -11 (rho = 20)
  Transcendental pair: α = 4 + i√105, |α|² = 121
  Trans char poly: f_11(T) = 1 - 8T + 121T²
  disc(f_11) = -420  (= -2²·3·5·7)
  Square class: -105

Square class ratio: -2 / -105 = 2/105  (square? 1)

DIFFERENT square classes!
If rho_{Qbar} = 20, then disc(NS_{Qbar}) would have to lie in both
square classes (image in both NS_{F_p}), which is impossible.
Therefore rho_{Qbar} <= 18.

Combined with rho_{Qbar} >= 16 from explicit classes:
  rho_{Qbar}(V'_min) ∈ {16, 18}.
